{"@context":{"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","course":"identifier","wing":"isBasedOn","title":"name","principle":"abstract","skills":"keywords","lessons":"hasPart","key":"identifier","why":"description","statement":"mathExpression","address":"sameAs","skill":"keywords"},"@type":"Course","kind":"school-course","course":"CrossFormulas","wing":"CrossFormulas.lean","title":"Cross Formulas","principle":"THE LEDGER'S OWN ALGEBRA, COUNTED (the captain, 2026-09-25","skills":["cross-formulas"],"level":100,"band":"100–999 steps","rank":95,"lessons":[{"key":"most_of_the_unstated_remainder_stays_inside_one_wing","name":"CLAIMED: of 99621 unstated crosses, 44690 join integers no single wing carries all three of — over two fifths and under a half, since 44690 × 5 > 99621 × 2 and 44690 × 2 < 99621.","statement":"((44690 * 5 > 99621 * 2) ∧ (44690 * 2 < 99621)) ∧ (44690 < 99621)","address":"37af5716-12b3-8249-95a1-89444b34dce0","skill":"cross-formulas","tex":"44690 \\cdot 5 > 99621 \\cdot 2 \\quad\\land\\quad 44690 \\cdot 2 < 99621 \\quad\\land\\quad 44690 < 99621","mathml":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>44690</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>99621</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mrow><mn>44690</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>99621</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mn>44690</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>99621</mn></mrow></mrow></math>","exercise":null},{"key":"the_corpus_has_sealed_under_a_hundredth_of_its_own_closure","name":"CLAIMED: the 1526 formula-shaped statements across 114 wings are built from 786 distinct integers and state 456 crosses, against 99621 their own integers generate unstated — fewer than one in a hundred, since 456 × 100 < 99621, at between 13 and 14 formulas per wing.","statement":"((456 * 100 < 99621) ∧ (786 > 114)) ∧ ((114 * 13 < 1526) ∧ (114 * 14 > 1526))","address":"d029da95-76be-8614-8cc0-cbdaa021c1bf","skill":"cross-formulas","tex":"456 \\cdot 100 < 99621 \\quad\\land\\quad 786 > 114 \\quad\\land\\quad 114 \\cdot 13 < 1526 \\quad\\land\\quad 114 \\cdot 14 > 1526","mathml":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>456</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>99621</mn></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mn>786</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>114</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>114</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1526</mn></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mrow><mn>114</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1526</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></math>","exercise":null},{"key":"the_generated_closure_partitions_into_three_kinds","name":"CLAIMED: of 621753 crosses the corpus's own integers generate, 521676 are forced by an operator's algebra, 456 are stated, and 99621 remain — and the three account for the whole, 521676 + 456 + 99621 = 621753.","statement":"(521676 + 456 + 99621 = 621753) ∧ (521676 > 5 * 99621)","address":"5256b67b-1195-83fe-a6cd-fd3630d8f135","skill":"cross-formulas","tex":"521676 + 456 + 99621 = 621753 \\quad\\land\\quad 521676 > 5 \\cdot 99621","mathml":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>521676</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>456</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>99621</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>621753</mn></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mn>521676</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>99621</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></math>","exercise":null},{"key":"the_same_arithmetic_answers_more_than_one_domain","name":"CLAIMED: 1526 formula-shaped statements carry 1454 distinct byte-strings and 1321 distinct algebraic forms, so 205 statements restate a form another already holds — and those split 129 copies against 15 crosses, the widest cross spanning 3 wings. Most repetition is waste; a small part of it is a bridge.","statement":"((1321 < 1454) ∧ (1526 - 1321 = 205)) ∧ ((129 > 8 * 15) ∧ (3 > 2))","address":"a6d91bd3-2737-8330-913e-e3cec9669ab6","skill":"cross-formulas","tex":"1321 < 1454 \\quad\\land\\quad 1526 - 1321 = 205 \\quad\\land\\quad 129 > 8 \\cdot 15 \\quad\\land\\quad 3 > 2","mathml":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1321</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1454</mn></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mrow><mn>1526</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1321</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>205</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mrow><mn>129</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></math>","exercise":null},{"key":"the_arithmetic_alphabet_partitions_by_its_own_algebra","name":"CLAIMED: the 6 arithmetic operators the sealed formulas use split by what they do over the corpus's own integers — 2 commutative (* +), 3 forced on their diagonal (% - /), 1 neither (^) — and 2 + 3 + 1 = 6.","statement":"((2 + 3 + 1 = 6) ∧ (3 > 2)) ∧ (2 > 1)","address":"23ada59a-4cdb-8039-94c3-cdd81827a5c2","skill":"cross-formulas","tex":"2 + 3 + 1 = 6 \\quad\\land\\quad 3 > 2 \\quad\\land\\quad 2 > 1","mathml":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>","exercise":null}],"exercises":0}
